Q) What do you mean hoisting not done same way (function vs function expression)
We read that about in "cheatsheet-this-day8"
Ans) 
a) Function declaration

sayHi(); // Works because function declarations are hoisted.
        //  whole function is available before the code line where it   appears.
function sayHi() {
  console.log("hi");
}

b) Function expression

sayHi();// doesn't work.ReferenceError: Cannot access 'sayHi' before initialization
        //  variable sayHi is hoisted but its value function () { ... } is not available yet i.e variable name is known early, not the function value.
       
const sayHi = function () {
  console.log("hi");
};

# One more example
console.log(a); // undefined
var a = 10; // With var, the variable is hoisted and initialized to undefined.

But with:

console.log(b); // ReferenceError
const b = 10;// b exists in a "temporal dead zone" until the declaration runs.

For functions same

## Short version
Function declaration: callable before its line
Function expression: variable exists earlier, but the function is not callable until assignment happens

Q) Hoisting quick reference
Ans)
// Hoisting quick reference

// 1) Function declaration
declarationFn();
function declarationFn() {
  console.log("function declaration");
}

// 2) Function expression with const
// expressionConst(); // ReferenceError
const expressionConst = function () {
  console.log("function expression with const");
};
expressionConst();

// 3) Function expression with var
expressionVar(); // TypeError: expressionVar is not a function .Intereting see explanation below "## Quick explanation"
var expressionVar = function () {
  console.log("function expression with var");
};

// 4) var variable
console.log(varValue); // undefined
var varValue = 10;

// 5) let variable
// console.log(letValue); // ReferenceError
let letValue = 20;

// 6) const variable
// console.log(constValue); // ReferenceError
const constValue = 30;

## Quick explanation
function declaration - fully hoisted; can be called before it appears

const / let - hoisted, but not initialized;cannot be used before declaration

var - hoisted and initialized to undefined
    - but function expressions assigned to var are not available yet
    
Important difference
var expressionVar = function () {};
Only expressionVar is hoisted, not the function body.

So this:

expressionVar();
fails because expressionVar is undefined at that moment.

